 17592186160099} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X \\le 14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160100} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X = 14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160101}] :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160098}] :question/math-academy-id "q-224421" :question/problem "A treatment for a particular medical condition has a $0.25$ probability of success. A pharmaceutical company launches a new drug for the condition and claims that the probability of success has improved. A doctor wishes to test the pharmaceutical company's claim, so he conducts a hypothesis test at the $5%$ significance level.\n\nAfter randomly sampling $50$ patients with the condition, the doctor observes that $14$ of them succeed with the new treatment. The doctor compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the following cumulative distribution table for $X∼B(50, 0.25)$.\n\n| $x$ | $12$ | $13$ | $14$ | $15$ | $16$ | $17$ | $18$ | $19$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.5110$ | $0.6370$ | $0.7481$ | $0.8369$ | $0.9017$ | $0.9449$ | $0.9713$ | $0.9861$ |\n\nFill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of randomly selected patients that are treated successfully, then a suitable test statistic is\n$X = 14$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSince $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $5%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, we {{field-6}} that the success rate has increased in patients treated with the new drug."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9995" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160103} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160104} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :a